    "note": [
        {
            "structuredValue": [],
            "parallelValue": [],
            "groupedValue": [],
            "value": "As sensory hair cells of the mammalian ear lack regenerative capacity, improper development or the loss of these cells can lead to permanent hearing loss. The Wnt pathway plays an important role in the proliferation and differentiation of sensory cells during cochlear development. As a feedback inhibitor of the Wnt pathway, Axin2 reliably reports Wnt-responsive cells. Prior to hair cell differentiation, Axin2-expressing cells are mainly found in the periotic mesenchyme. Although the function of these cells is not well understood, we have shown in in vitro experiments that Wnt agonists and antagonists can regulate the proliferative capacity of Axin2-expressing cells from the late embryonic period. Furthermore, lineage tracing experiments suggest that Axin2-expressing cells can act as precursors to sensory hair cells and adjacent supporting cells. Currently, the biological relevance of these cells and the mechanisms through which they may contribute to cochlear development are unknown. We used transgenic mice to stabilize beta-catenin by deleting exon3, which codes the region important for its degradation, thus augmenting Wnt signaling in Axin2-expressing cells prior to hair cell differentiation. The effects of this manipulation were characterized using markers of proliferation, epithelial cells, mesenchymal cells, hair cells, and supporting cells on whole mounts and cryosections of cochlear tissues obtained one week after activation. Beta-catenin stabilization leads to the formation of proliferative foci in the cochlear duct, with a low but significant percentage of cells displaying markers of cochlear sensory epithelial cells. \r\n     These results demonstrate that Axin2-expressing cells can proliferate in response to beta-catenin stabilization and may act as precursors to cochlear ductal cells during cochlear development. This work increases our understanding of the roles of Wnt signaling during cochlear development and will be important in progress towards hair cell regeneration.",
